1
00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:05.472
In this lesson, you will optimize
an existing vector

2
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:08.608
search mechanism
to reduce its memory usage.

3
00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:14.147
You will explore different types
of vector quantization that not only make

4
00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:18.918
semantic search more affordable,
but can also enhance its efficiency.

5
00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.789
Let's code and innovate.

6
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:26.760
You might be surprised to see
how effective the vectors may still be,

7
00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:30.096
even if we compress them
using various quantization techniques.

8
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.165
Let's discuss the basics.

9
00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:35.502
Vector embeddings are usually represented
by floating point numbers.

10
00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:39.506
Float
32 means that each individual dimension

11
00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:43.376
needs 32 bits of memory,
which is equivalent to four bytes.

12
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.446
For example,
a popular OpenAI embedding model

13
00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:50.016
will produce over 1500 numbers
for each input text.

14
00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:55.183
That's six kilobytes of memory
for such an individual chunk in your RAG,

15
00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:00.093
and there's still some overhead generated
by the vector database structures.

16
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.731
This number has to be multiplied by
the total number of documents you will store.

17
00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:06.833
Even on a million scale,

18
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.836
there has to be a decent amount
of memory available.

19
00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.908
But if we go beyond that, the customer
grade machine won't handle that load.

20
00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.945
Putting the data on disk
was the only solution for those hitting

21
00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.613
their budget limits in the past.

22
00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:24.150
Quantization techniques
turned out to be much more powerful,

23
00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.987
and offered a way to make semantic search
more affordable,

24
00:01:28.321 --> 00:01:30.790
with a little impact
on the search quality.

25
00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.827
Product quantization is the first method
you will learn today.

26
00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:37.530
Everything starts with the original float
based embeddings.

27
00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:41.401
Product quantization
divides them into sub-vectors.

28
00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:45.371
The number of sub-vectors
depends on the selected compression rate.

29
00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:50.110
For example, a compression rate of 16
means each sub-vector will

30
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.114
have four dimensions, as the compression
rate is defined in bytes.

31
00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:55.548
Once the

32
00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:59.319
vectors are split into pieces,
we can start the compression.

33
00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.722
In the next step,
we start from the sub-vectors.

34
00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:06.092
Each group of sub
vectors is used as an input

35
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:09.395
to the clustering algorithm,
such as k means.

36
00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:14.167
Eventually, each sub-vector
will get assigned to the closest centroid.

37
00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:18.004
The number of centroids
is not configurable as it is always

38
00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:21.374
set to 256.

39
00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:26.446
A total number of possible values
we can represent with a single byte.

40
00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.350
From now
on, instead of storing the sub-vectors,

41
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.520
we keep the identifiers
of the closest centroids.

42
00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:37.157
In our case, each
sub-vector had four bytes,

43
00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:40.360
so it required 16 bytes overall.

44
00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:44.230
After the product quantization,
we just have to store

45
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.700
a single byte,
which is the centroid identifier.

46
00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:51.271
Sixteen bytes became just a single byte,

47
00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.707
so the achieved compression
rate is also 16.

48
00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:58.044
Product quantization is called product
because it divides

49
00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:02.749
a high dimensional vector into smaller sub
vectors, and quantize

50
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:06.152
each sub-vector
separately using its own codebook.

51
00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:09.589
The combination of this quantized sub
vectors

52
00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:12.859
forms a product of the individual code
books,

53
00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:16.696
creating a comprehensive product
code for the entire space.

54
00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.167
This approach leverages
the Cartesian product of the subspaces

55
00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:24.804
to efficiently approximate
the original high dimensional data.

56
00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.941
There are multiple possible
configurations of the product

57
00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:32.512
quantization and its impact vary
depending on how you set it up.

58
00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:36.449
If you are on a budget, then
you can consider setting the compression

59
00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:41.449
rate even up to 64 times,
but it's rarely works precisely enough.

60
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:46.921
In our case, the most aggressive
compression of 32 times decreased

61
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.763
the precision of search to around
0.66.

62
00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:54.234
Even though the search
with original vectors

63
00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:57.270
was able to get around 0.98,

64
00:03:58.338 --> 00:03:59.772
that's a huge difference.

65
00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:03.843
Product quantization will always result
in reduced search precision,

66
00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:07.513
but sometimes it may speed
things up a little bit.

67
00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.650
Inserting your points and building
the collection

68
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.087
will always be slower,
though, as it has to run

69
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.922
the K-means clustering.

70
00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:19.058
The degraded quality of quantization
may be improved back

71
00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.493
with the help of free-scoring.

72
00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:21.628
First of all,

73
00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:26.165
the quantized representations encode
very similar vectors in exactly

74
00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:30.069
the same way as their sub-vectors
get the same centroids

75
00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:34.407
assigned. That makes it harder
to always get the best results possible,

76
00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:38.845
as many points might be now represented
with the same coordinates.

77
00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:42.348
The same coordinates
also means the same scores.

78
00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:46.386
For that reason, vector databases
keep the original vectors on disk.

79
00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.390
Once we have a set of results
returned by quantized search,

80
00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:55.228
we can load the original embeddings
and compute the similarity using them.

81
00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.798
That helps to differentiate the distances

82
00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:02.068
between the points
coming from the same neighborhood.

83
00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.104
Restoring
should improve the quality of search

84
00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:08.641
and choosing to turn it off
should be rather a conscious decision.

85
00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:12.979
Let's see how product quantization
works for our dataset.

86
00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:16.983
In order to test the impact
of quantization on the search quality,

87
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.920
you have to import the snapshot
of the once collection again.

88
00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:29.362
Let's also repeat the process
of calculating query embeddings

89
00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:31.030
with our selected model.

90
00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:36.030
Whenever we speak about measuring
quality, we have to random.

91
00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:44.544
You already know we
cannot improve the embedding model quality

92
00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.547
by applying any technique
to the created vectors.

93
00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:52.613
Therefore, our baseline is defined by the
outputs of the exact knn search,

94
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.923
similarly to the previous lesson.

95
00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:01.928
You are now ready

96
00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:05.932
to evaluate the impact of the product
quantization on the search quality.

97
00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.403
You have to modify the configuration
of the collection

98
00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:14.040
to enable the product
quantization on its vectors.

99
00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:18.444
This change of the configuration
will fire the quantization process

100
00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:21.881
and then the whole collection
will be rebuilt.

101
00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:25.518
There is no downtime,
but during that changes been applied,

102
00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:29.422
you might be still using
the old structures or exact search.

103
00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:32.625
From a user perspective, nothing changes.

104
00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.292
Setting

105
00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:37.597
always run property forces Qdrants
to keep the quantized vectors

106
00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:39.832
always in memory.

107
00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:44.137
Again, let's wait for the optimizer
to finish its job.

108
00:06:44.504 --> 00:06:47.573
We want to measure the impact
of product quantization.

109
00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.342
So let's wait until it's ready.

110
00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:57.083
You will

111
00:06:57.083 --> 00:07:00.520
be now able to evaluate the quality
of the collection again.

112
00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:04.490
The process is not different
from what we have done already.

113
00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.693
When we did that for different actions
HSNW configurations.

114
00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:16.769
The only thing we add here is an operation
that measures

115
00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:20.673
how much time we spent
on each search operation.

116
00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:26.073
Eventually, you will display the average
latency, as it is also an important factor

117
00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:29.549
when choosing the right parameters
for your semantic search.

118
00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:34.854
In this initial test,

119
00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:39.826
we disable the Rescoring mechanism
for each quantization technique.

120
00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:44.764
You will run evaluate twice,
either with or without the rescoring.

121
00:07:45.565 --> 00:07:48.568
Obviously rescoring comes
with an additional cost,

122
00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:51.571
so it's better to avoid it if possible.

123
00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:55.341
Finally, we will store the latency

124
00:07:55.775 --> 00:08:00.146
of the search requests
and then build a dictionary.

125
00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:03.583
Similarity to what we did
for all the other tests so far.

126
00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:06.118
This is the average

127
00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:09.222
response time for product quantization

128
00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:12.725
without the rescoring,

129
00:08:12.725 --> 00:08:15.661
The product quantization
results are also ready.

130
00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:20.566
Let's evaluate their quality versus
the exact search than on original float

131
00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:24.036
based vectors. Again,
using precision at 25.

132
00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.273
But you can also experiment
with different values.

133
00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:30.510
Product

134
00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:35.147
quantization reduced the search precision,
but we got pretty aggressive

135
00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:38.351
and requested the compression
ratio of 64 times.

136
00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:41.988
Let's repeat the process of evaluation,

137
00:08:41.988 --> 00:08:44.991
but this time, with the rescoring enabled.

138
00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:48.761
It will recompute the distances
using original vectors,

139
00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:51.731
and that should hopefully improve
the quality.

140
00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:57.837
There is just a single difference
in how we evaluate the quality

141
00:08:57.837 --> 00:09:02.675
in this case. We enable the rescoring
for all the search queries we set,

142
00:09:03.476 --> 00:09:06.479
but all the rest is kept identical.

143
00:09:09.048 --> 00:09:12.084
Now let's run ranx evaluation again.

144
00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.089
Rescoring improves the quality of search,

145
00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:20.192
as different documents
with the same representation

146
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.564
may have their scores computed properly
with full precision.

147
00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:28.234
Let's review
the second quantization technique

148
00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:31.237
available for vector search.

149
00:09:31.637 --> 00:09:33.773
Scalar quantization is another technique

150
00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:37.276
you may apply to make semantic search
less memory intensive.

151
00:09:37.977 --> 00:09:42.715
It is based on the very simple idea
of converting floats into integers.

152
00:09:43.382 --> 00:09:47.353
However, when we have the original vectors
represented with floats,

153
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.090
the individual numbers
come from a specific range,

154
00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:55.461
which is just a subset of all the values
that we may represent with floats.

155
00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:59.165
When we then receive some new vectors,
the range may change,

156
00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:01.968
so we never know the minimum
and maximum values.

157
00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:05.871
That's why quantization is done
separately on subsets of the data,

158
00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:08.574
not globally for all the vectors.

159
00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:11.677
Do you remember that
HSNW graph is split into segments?

160
00:10:12.144 --> 00:10:15.181
That also helps
to implement Scalar quantization.

161
00:10:15.548 --> 00:10:19.151
As the database assumes,
each segment is immutable.

162
00:10:19.785 --> 00:10:22.221
So we can measure the range of the numbers

163
00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:25.224
and calculate minimum and maximum values.

164
00:10:25.391 --> 00:10:29.562
Then the whole quantization process
is about converting these numbers

165
00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:33.265
from the range of integers between 0

166
00:10:33.265 --> 00:10:38.265
to 255, or -128 to 127.

167
00:10:39.739 --> 00:10:43.109
The compression
rate of Scalar quantization is always

168
00:10:43.109 --> 00:10:46.545
four. Four bytes are compressed
to a single one.

169
00:10:46.879 --> 00:10:50.249
The memory required to keep the vectors
after applying

170
00:10:50.249 --> 00:10:53.619
scalar
quantization is slower by up to 75%.

171
00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:57.223
That comes with a cost
in terms of search precision,

172
00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.192
but there are also speed benefits.

173
00:11:00.192 --> 00:11:04.030
Overall, Scalar quantization
is usually the first thing to consider

174
00:11:04.296 --> 00:11:07.833
if you want to make your search faster
and more affordable.

175
00:11:08.768 --> 00:11:11.837
Let's consider scalar quantization
on the collection we have

176
00:11:12.071 --> 00:11:15.174
and test
how it impacts the quality of retrieval.

177
00:11:15.675 --> 00:11:18.678
You will now repeat
the quality evaluation process.

178
00:11:19.211 --> 00:11:24.050
But on the results provided by Scalar
quantization. It has to be enabled first.

179
00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:26.752
But all the other steps are similar.

180
00:11:31.323 --> 00:11:34.326
Qdrant
has to finish the rebuilding process.

181
00:11:34.393 --> 00:11:37.396
So let's give it some time.

182
00:11:44.804 --> 00:11:46.906
Our first attempt to evaluate Scalar

183
00:11:46.906 --> 00:11:49.909
quantization will be performed
without the rescoring.

184
00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:56.649
Ranx
require is to have a run object created.

185
00:11:56.649 --> 00:11:59.652
Let's do it.

186
00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:04.323
It will also evaluate
the same metric on the collection

187
00:12:04.323 --> 00:12:07.727
with Scalar quantization enabled,
but this time

188
00:12:08.027 --> 00:12:11.030
with rescoring.

189
00:12:14.033 --> 00:12:17.036
Let's run the ranx evaluation again.

190
00:12:19.205 --> 00:12:21.874
We've just calculated the precision at 25

191
00:12:21.874 --> 00:12:26.278
for both rescoring disabled
and enabled on the same collection.

192
00:12:27.146 --> 00:12:30.449
We'll compare all the quantization
methods at the very end.

193
00:12:31.016 --> 00:12:34.253
Last but not least,
Binary quantization takes

194
00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:37.690
the idea of compressing
individual dimensions even further.

195
00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.793
It converts each floating point number

196
00:12:40.793 --> 00:12:43.796
into a boolean, either 0 or 1.

197
00:12:44.463 --> 00:12:47.333
32 bits of information

198
00:12:47.333 --> 00:12:50.202
compressed to just a single bit.

199
00:12:50.202 --> 00:12:53.105
The role is as simple as converting
each positive value

200
00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:56.542
to one, and each negative or 0 to 0.

201
00:12:57.009 --> 00:13:00.579
One important thing in binary
quantization is over-sampling.

202
00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:05.751
Since many points will be represented
in exactly the same way.

203
00:13:06.185 --> 00:13:09.455
You want to extract more points
that you really need,

204
00:13:09.889 --> 00:13:13.025
and then calculate the distance
between your query

205
00:13:13.325 --> 00:13:16.328
and original vectors
represented as floats.

206
00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:20.399
Again, let's see the real-world
impact of the binary quantization.

207
00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:25.237
Your last attempt to quantize
vectors will focus on binary quantization.

208
00:13:25.905 --> 00:13:30.576
Enabling it is as simple as changing
the collection configuration again

209
00:13:31.143 --> 00:13:33.846
and setting the quantization
config to binary.

210
00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:39.084
Qdrant will

211
00:13:39.084 --> 00:13:41.620
remove the scalar quantized vectors

212
00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:45.090
and start the binary quantization
on the original ones.

213
00:13:45.457 --> 00:13:48.460
Let's give it some time
to finish that operation.

214
00:13:54.099 --> 00:13:57.503
At this point, you should already know
what we will do next.

215
00:13:58.137 --> 00:14:01.407
Let's run our benchmark on the collection

216
00:14:01.407 --> 00:14:06.378
with binary quantization enabled
and obviously do not rescore

217
00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:09.682
in the first test.

218
00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:14.453
Do you want to see the impact
on our metric?

219
00:14:14.687 --> 00:14:17.690
Let's calculate this.

220
00:14:17.957 --> 00:14:18.624
Again.

221
00:14:18.624 --> 00:14:21.527
Let's enable
rescoring and run all the queries

222
00:14:21.527 --> 00:14:24.530
against our collection.

223
00:14:26.465 --> 00:14:29.635
Finally, the impact of the rescoring

224
00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:33.405
on the binary quantization
also has to be determined.

225
00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:37.543
As a last step,

226
00:14:37.543 --> 00:14:40.279
you will compare the search precision

227
00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:43.282
achieved by all the tested methods.

228
00:14:43.315 --> 00:14:45.718
As you may see, the highest results

229
00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:48.721
were achieved by Scalar quantization.

230
00:14:49.154 --> 00:14:52.324
Either
a with or without rescoring enabled.

231
00:14:53.559 --> 00:14:55.261
Binary quantization

232
00:14:55.261 --> 00:14:57.963
with additional rescoring interface,

233
00:14:57.963 --> 00:15:00.966
also did the job well.

234
00:15:01.133 --> 00:15:05.237
However, choosing the right quantization
model depends on the specific

235
00:15:05.237 --> 00:15:09.008
requirements of your project
and the models you use.

236
00:15:09.608 --> 00:15:14.213
Quantization is usually the first method
to consider when you want

237
00:15:14.213 --> 00:15:18.851
to make your semantic search faster,
without sacrificing quality too much.

238
00:15:19.618 --> 00:15:24.490
Binary quantization offers reduced
memory usage by even up to 32 times.

239
00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:29.161
However, the performance benefits
might be even higher,

240
00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:33.732
as it can increase the speed of retrieval
by up to 40 times. Overall,

241
00:15:33.866 --> 00:15:37.903
choosing the right quantization technique
depends on the specifics

242
00:15:38.137 --> 00:15:40.239
of your applications.

243
00:15:40.239 --> 00:15:45.044
From now on, you should know
what are the options and testing them out

